_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Package installation, database initialization and service enablement, database creation/import, and sequence repair were necessary task actions. Transactional allocation tests temporarily advanced the nontransactional sequence but were bounded evidence collection and explicitly restored it to 100. The reboot served the mandatory restart protocol. Final observations show no failed services, healthy package state, and PostgreSQL listening only on loopback; its socket files and service-related temporary directories are expected operational state rather than residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9d22d722d1a84219","cmd-a51a831043c34a7e","cmd-c05931e137eb4ef8","cmd-c57e61c4476043d4","cmd-544e0e1df1e84de8","cmd-bc3adc641b9742fe","cmd-8018f8a58c6348b0","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide node-restart protocol is also complete and coherent: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, restart scheduling was acknowledged, a different boot ID was observed, and the final system showed PostgreSQL active and ready with the database, rows, sequence behavior, source export, OS, and kernel recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["Only one implicit allocation was exercised per bounded transaction; continued uniqueness is additionally supported by the observed SERIAL sequence, primary key, repaired sequence state, and identical successful behavior after restart."]}
